          "outcome_id": "BO-03",
          "text": "Relate x^T x to the squared Euclidean norm without classifying general quadratic forms."
        }
      ],
      "teaching_content": [
        {
          "content_id": "BC-01",
          "kind": "definition",
          "text": "For real vectors x and y of the same length, x^T y is the dot product and is the scalar sum of componentwise products.",
          "source_ids": [
            "SRC-01"
          ]
        },
        {
          "content_id": "BC-02",
          "kind": "procedure",
          "text": "To compute x^T A x, first compute y = Ax and then compute the scalar x^T y. Check that A is square and compatible with x.",
          "source_ids": [
            "SRC-02"
          ]
        },
        {
          "content_id": "BC-03",
          "kind": "explanation",
          "text": "The special case x^T x equals ||x||_2^2 and is non-negative.",
          "source_ids": [
            "SRC-01"
          ]
        },
        {
          "content_id": "BC-04",
          "kind": "worked_example",
          "text": "For A = [[2, 0], [0, 1]] and x = (1, 2)^T, Ax = (2, 2)^T and x^T A x = 1*2 + 2*2 = 6.",
          "source_ids": [
            "SRC-02"
          ]
        }
